  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Member based organizations continue to lose members, struggle to collect subscriptions regularly and to respond to issues related to the informal economy. The social partners face serious financial problems and with donor fatigue and affiliate unions also facing serious problems due to the collapse of industry, resulting in unions failing to finance their operations. There is also competition among trade union centres for membership and social and political influence. The on-going labour law reform process and the efforts to institutionalise dialogue under the Tripartite Negotiating Forum present opportunities for social partners to lobby and advocate for mainstreaming workers&#x2019; rights into revised labour laws in line with key international labour standards. Key issues include the need to build and strengthen capacities of trade unions: representation including in the informal economy, freedom of association and collective bargaining; termination of employment, extension of social security to workers in precarious employment the majority of which are women and unjustified dismissals; strategic planning, resource mobilisation and the need for workers to influence policy agendas relevant to workers&#x2019; rights at national, regional and international levels.</narrative>
  </description>
